type: software
title: "Study app, data, analysis code and materials for: What's the Difference? Voice Design and Confirmation Bias in AI-Assisted Self-Diagnosis: Anthropomorphic vs. Machine-Like Voice Output"
authors:
  - given-names: Niklas
    family-names: Röthlingshöfer
    affiliation: Technische Hochschule Ingolstadt
  - given-names: Emma
    family-names: Winkler
    affiliation: Technische Hochschule Ingolstadt
  - given-names: Melanie
    family-names: Löffler
    affiliation: Technische Hochschule Ingolstadt
  - given-names: Emely
    family-names: Rosbach
    affiliation: Technische Hochschule Ingolstadt
abstract: >-
  Study application, pseudonymised data, analysis code and materials for a
  within-subjects online experiment (N = 81, 648 trials) on whether the voice design
  of an AI symptom checker moderates confirmation bias in lay self-diagnosis.
  Participants heard an AI recommendation that confirmed or refuted their initial
  diagnosis, spoken by an anthropomorphic or a machine-like voice, and then decided
  again. The direction of the recommendation affected decisions, and the voice did
  not moderate this effect.
keywords:
  - confirmation bias
  - automation bias
  - anthropomorphism
  - voice user interface
  - text-to-speech
  - AI symptom checker
  - self-diagnosis
  - human-AI interaction
  - trust in AI
  - healthcare AI
  - null result
  - equivalence testing
